{{also|Syllepsis}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|syllepsis}}, from {{der|en|grc|σύλληψις}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|GA}} {{IPA|en|/sɪˈlɛp.sɪs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-syllepsis.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{examples|sense=rhetoric|examples=
* She lowered her standards by '''raising''' her glass, Her courage, her eyes and his hopes.
*: – Flanders and Swann
* Here thou, great Anna! whom three realms obey, Dost sometimes counsel '''take'''—and sometimes tea.
*: – Alexander Pope
* She '''cribbed''' the baby and then the corn.
* She came '''in''' high spirits and a Cadillac.}}
{{en-noun|~|syllepses}}

# {{lb|en|rhetoric}} A [[figure of speech]] in which one [[word]] [[simultaneously]] [[modifies]] two or more other words such that the modification must be [[understood]] [[differently]] with respect to each modified word; often causing [[humorous]] [[incongruity]].
#: {{cot|en|zeugma}}
#: {{hyper|en|brachylogy}}
# {{lb|en|botany}} [[growth|Growth]] in which [[lateral]] [[branch]]es develop from a lateral [[meristem]], without the formation of a [[bud]] or period of [[dormancy]], when the lateral meristem is split from a [[terminal]] meristem.
#: {{ant|en|prolepsis}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|sylleptic}}

